{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-32533" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]$\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then ${l}_{32}\\cdot {u}_{33}=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069826/state.json" "file_sha256" "b73e7e81e0fea47c1a6e62b729081f627ce063945538b6e791b9bfcb4bfd55f2"} {"question" "q-32533" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-0.5){R}_{2} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{2}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -0.5 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot {E}_{1}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}A & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -0.5 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & =([\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0.5 & 1\\end{aligned}])U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0.5 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{32}=0.5,{u}_{33}=1,$ and ${l}_{32}\\cdot {u}_{33}=0.5.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069826/state.json" "file_sha256" "b73e7e81e0fea47c1a6e62b729081f627ce063945538b6e791b9bfcb4bfd55f2"} {"question" "q-32533" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069826/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4e1f1c1e81892c4d56976feff83c2f8efe47113a042e3674b1229c69cfdee3f1"} {"question" "q-32533" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069826/state.json" "file_sha256" "b73e7e81e0fea47c1a6e62b729081f627ce063945538b6e791b9bfcb4bfd55f2"} {"question" "q-32533" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-0.5"] ["math" "-1"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "0.5"] ["math" "2"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069826/state.json" "file_sha256" "b73e7e81e0fea47c1a6e62b729081f627ce063945538b6e791b9bfcb4bfd55f2"} {"question" "q-32533" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.5"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069826/state.json" "file_sha256" "b73e7e81e0fea47c1a6e62b729081f627ce063945538b6e791b9bfcb4bfd55f2"} {"question" "q-32722" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that\n$K=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 3\\end{aligned}]$\nhas the LU decomposition $K=LU,$ then $L=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069826/state.json" "file_sha256" "b73e7e81e0fea47c1a6e62b729081f627ce063945538b6e791b9bfcb4bfd55f2"} {"question" "q-32722" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $K$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $K$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}K & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{7}{4}){R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{7}{4} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot K \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -7 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{2} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{2}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot {E}_{1}\\cdot K \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -7 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}K=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}K & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}K & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ K & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ K & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{7}{4} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ K & =([\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{7}{4} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}])U \\\\ K & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{7}{4} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}4 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -7 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069826/state.json" "file_sha256" "b73e7e81e0fea47c1a6e62b729081f627ce063945538b6e791b9bfcb4bfd55f2"} {"question" "q-32722" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069826/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4e1f1c1e81892c4d56976feff83c2f8efe47113a042e3674b1229c69cfdee3f1"} {"question" "q-32722" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069826/state.json" "file_sha256" "b73e7e81e0fea47c1a6e62b729081f627ce063945538b6e791b9bfcb4bfd55f2"} {"question" "q-32722" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{7}{4} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{7}{4} & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{7}{4} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{7}{4} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{7}{4} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069826/state.json" "file_sha256" "b73e7e81e0fea47c1a6e62b729081f627ce063945538b6e791b9bfcb4bfd55f2"} {"question" "q-32722" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{7}{4} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069826/state.json" "file_sha256" "b73e7e81e0fea47c1a6e62b729081f627ce063945538b6e791b9bfcb4bfd55f2"} {"question" "q-106849" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose that a $3\\times 3$ matrix $H$ is reduced to row echelon form using the following elementary row operations, applied in the order in which they appear:\n\n$$\n{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-4{R}_{1}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $H$ has the LU factorization $H=LU,$ then $L=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069826/state.json" "file_sha256" "b73e7e81e0fea47c1a6e62b729081f627ce063945538b6e791b9bfcb4bfd55f2"} {"question" "q-106849" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Applying ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+1\\cdot {R}_{1}$ and then ${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-4)\\cdot {R}_{1}$ is equivalent to multiplying $H$ from the left by the following elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{E}_{2}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{E}_{1}}\\cdot H=U\n$$\n\nNow, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}H=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}H & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}H & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ H & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ H & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ H & =([\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}])U \\\\ H & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}\\cdot U\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069826/state.json" "file_sha256" "b73e7e81e0fea47c1a6e62b729081f627ce063945538b6e791b9bfcb4bfd55f2"} {"question" "q-106849" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069826/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4e1f1c1e81892c4d56976feff83c2f8efe47113a042e3674b1229c69cfdee3f1"} {"question" "q-106849" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069826/state.json" "file_sha256" "b73e7e81e0fea47c1a6e62b729081f627ce063945538b6e791b9bfcb4bfd55f2"} {"question" "q-106849" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069826/state.json" "file_sha256" "b73e7e81e0fea47c1a6e62b729081f627ce063945538b6e791b9bfcb4bfd55f2"} {"question" "q-106849" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069826/state.json" "file_sha256" "b73e7e81e0fea47c1a6e62b729081f627ce063945538b6e791b9bfcb4bfd55f2"} {"question" "q-106850" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose that a $3\\times 3$ matrix $C$ is reduced to row echelon form using the following elementary row operations, applied in the order in which they appear:\n\n$$\n{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-6{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+9{R}_{1}\n$$\n\nGiven that $C$ has the LU factorization $C=LU,$ then $L=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069826/state.json" "file_sha256" "b73e7e81e0fea47c1a6e62b729081f627ce063945538b6e791b9bfcb4bfd55f2"} {"question" "q-106850" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Applying ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-6)\\cdot {R}_{1}$ and then ${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+9\\cdot {R}_{1}$ is equivalent to multiplying $C$ from the left by the following elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 9 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{E}_{2}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{E}_{1}}\\cdot C=U\n$$\n\nNow, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}C=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}C & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}C & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ C & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ C & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 9 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ C & =([\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -9 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}])U \\\\ C & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -9 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}\\cdot U\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069826/state.json" "file_sha256" "b73e7e81e0fea47c1a6e62b7